WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 335: Queen Elizabeth II (Part 2) & King Charles III: Annus Horribilis, Diana, the Platinum Jubilee, and the Accession of Charles III

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:13.680
[Rebecca] Today, we conclude our epic series on the kings and queens of England with the climactic story of our contemporary era.

4
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.720
[Rebecca] The stormy later decades and record-breaking platinum jubilee of Queen Elizabeth I.

5
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.680
[Rebecca] And the historic accession of King Charles I.

6
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:29.760
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary finale of the Thousand Year Royal Story.

7
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.120
[George] On July 29, 1981, St.

8
00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:39.920
[George] Paul's Cathedral in London was the setting for what the Archbishop of Canterbury hailed as the stuff of which fairy tales are made.

9
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:46.720
[George] The wedding of 32-year-old Prince Charles, Prince of Wales, to 19-year-old Lady Diana Spencer.

10
00:00:46.720 --> 00:01:04.640
[George] With a global television audience exceeding 750 million people, Diana stepped out of the glass coach in an ivory taffeta gown with a breathtaking 25-foot train, capturing the romantic imagination of the world and injecting irresistible glamour into the British royal family.

11
00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:18.240
[George] Diana quickly became the most photographed woman in human history, using her magnetic star power to break down barriers, famously touching Aides' patients with bare hands and walking through active landmine fields in Angola.

12
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.440
[George] Yet beneath the glittering public facade, the royal marriage was a tragic mismatch.

13
00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:34.080
[George] Prince Charles remained deeply emotionally attached to his former flame, Camilla Parker Bowles, while Diana struggled with severe bulimia, isolation, and depression.

14
00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:42.160
[George] By the late 1980s, the marriage had completely disintegrated into a bitter, leaks-driven war conducted through the British tabloids.

15
00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:48.160
[George] In 1992, the publication of Andrew Morton's sensational biography, Diana.

16
00:01:48.160 --> 00:02:02.360
[George] Her true story, written with Diana's secret taped collaboration, exposed the Prince of Wales's infidelity and the Duchess of York's financial scandals, exposing the royal household to unprecedented public scrutiny and ridicule.

17
00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:06.680
[George] In June 1982, Diana gave birth to Prince William at St.

18
00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.000
[George] Mary's Hospital, Paddington.

19
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:19.000
[George] The first time an heir apparent to the British throne was born in a public hospital rather than behind royal palace walls, a modern precedent that reshaped royal childrearing.

20
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:33.800
[George] Yet the intensifying tabloid feeding frenzy surrounding Diana's every movement, wardrobe choice, and emotional state eclipsed the traditional functions of the monarchy, turning ancient royal duties into the world's most scrutinized soap opera.

21
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:41.320
[George] The year 1992 proved to be the darkest personal and constitutional year of Queen Elizabeth I's entire reign.

22
00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:49.000
[George] In March, her second son, Prince Andrew, Duke of York, separated from his wife Sarah Ferguson, in April.

23
00:02:49.000 --> 00:03:01.160
[George] Her daughter Princess Anne was divorced from Captain Mark Phillips, and in December, Prime Minister John Major officially announced to the House of Commons that the Prince and Princess of Wales had agreed to separate.

24
00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:10.920
[George] Then, on the morning of November 20, 1992, the Queen's 45th wedding anniversary, disaster struck the ancestral home of the monarchy.

25
00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:16.520
[George] A faulty spotlight ignited a curtain in Queen Victoria's private chapel at Windsor Castle.

26
00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:24.760
[George] The blaze spread with terrifying fury through the medieval timber roofs, raging for fifteen hours and destroying Brunswick Tower, St.

27
00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:28.280
[George] George's Hall, and over one hundred historic rooms.

28
00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:41.160
[George] Prince Andrew personally helped firefighters form human chains to rescue irreplaceable Renaissance masterpieces, while the Queen walked through the smoking ruins in a raincoat and Wellington boots, visibly devastated.

29
00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:48.880
[George] Four days later, at a guildhall luncheon marking her ruby jubilee, the Queen delivered an emotional, historic speech.

30
00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:53.680
[George] 1992 is not a year on which I shall look back with undiluted pleasure.

31
00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.040
[George] In the words of one of my more sympathetic correspondents.

32
00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.080
[George] It has turned out to be an Annis Horabilis.

33
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:05.280
[George] To pay for the castle's 70 million pound restoration without burdening taxpayers.

34
00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:14.400
[George] The Queen took the historic decision to open Buckingham Palace to paying tourists and voluntarily began paying personal income tax on her private wealth.

35
00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:23.040
[George] In the early hours of Sunday, August 31, 1997, the world was stunned by catastrophic news from Paris.

36
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:34.640
[George] Diana, Princess of Wales, had been killed in a high-speed car crash in the Pont de El Alma Tunnel alongside her companion Dodie Fade while fleeing pursuing paparazzi photographers.

37
00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:41.520
[George] Across Great Britain and the world, the shock provoked an unprecedented, volcanic eruption of public grief.

38
00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:51.200
[George] Millions of sobbing mourners laid over a million bouquets of flowers outside Kensington and Buckingham palaces, creating a fragrant floral sea five feet deep.

39
00:04:51.200 --> 00:05:08.960
[George] At Balmoral Castle in Scotland, Queen Elizabeth made the deliberate, protective decision to shield her grieving young grandsons, fifteen-year-old Prince William and twelve-year-old Prince Harry, keeping them isolated from hysterical media coverage and attending church services with them.

40
00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:27.520
[George] But in London, the royal family's silence and the absence of a royal standard at half-mast over Buckingham Palace, strictly in accordance with royal protocol, which dictates the sovereign's standard only flies when the monarch is in residence and never at half-mast, provoked savage front-page tabloid attacks.

41
00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.000
[George] Show us you care, screamed the headlines.

42
00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:38.760
[George] Recognizing the perilous rift opening between the Crown and her people, Prime Minister Tony Blair urged the Queen to return to London.

43
00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:49.960
[George] On September 5, 1997, Elizabeth returned to Buckingham Palace, walked among the tearful crowds inspecting the flowers, and addressed the nation live on television.

44
00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:52.680
[George] I want to pay tribute to Diana myself.

45
00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.560
[George] She was an exceptional and gifted human being.

46
00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:59.720
[George] I speak to you from my heart, as your queen and as a grandmother.

47
00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:15.960
[George] At Diana's funeral the next morning, as the gun carriage carrying the coffin passed Buckingham Palace, Queen Elizabeth bowed her head in solemn respect, an unprecedented royal gesture that diffused national anger and saved the monarchy from Republican backlash.

48
00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:21.000
[George] The early 2000s marked a magnificent personal resurrection for Queen Elizabeth I.

49
00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:27.400
[George] The year 2002 began with profound personal grief.

50
00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:44.120
[George] Within the space of seven weeks, the Queen lost both her beloved younger sister, Princess Margaret, who died in February at age 71, and her indomitable centenarian mother, Queen Elizabeth the Queen Mother, who passed away in March at age 101.

51
00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:49.960
[George] Yet the Queen's Golden Jubilee in June 2002 became a glorious national triumph.

52
00:06:49.960 --> 00:07:00.360
[George] Over a million people packed them all for a rock concert at Buckingham Palace, headlined by Paul McCartney and Queen's Brian May, playing God Save the Queen from the Palace Roof.

53
00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:01.800
[George] Ten years later.

54
00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:13.960
[George] In June 2012, the Diamond Jubilee celebrated sixty magnificent years on the throne, highlighted by a majestic pageant of 1,000 boats sailing down the River Thames through pouring rain.

55
00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:19.760
[George] As the 86-year-old Queen stood on the royal barge for four hours without sitting down.

56
00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:36.240
[George] Later that summer, the Queen delighted global audiences at the opening ceremony of the London 2012 Olympic Games, starring in a hilarious secret comedy sketch alongside Daniel Craig's James Bond, appearing to parachute out of a helicopter into the Olympic Stadium.

57
00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:47.200
[George] With Prince Charles happily married to Camilla and Prince William marrying Catherine Middleton in 2011, the House of Windsor had achieved unprecedented stability and global affection.

58
00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:51.600
[George] The secret Olympic sketch with Daniel Craig was the Queen's own idea.

59
00:07:51.600 --> 00:08:04.960
[George] When director Danny Boyle approached the palace for permission to film in the royal private apartments, the Queen insisted on having a speaking line rather than merely walking past the camera, delivering the iconic Good evening, Mr.

60
00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:16.400
[George] Bond with deadpan comedic perfection accompanied by her beloved Corgies, Monty and Willow, delighting an estimated worldwide television audience of 900 million people.

61
00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:25.120
[George] Throughout 73 years of marriage, Prince Philip had been Queen Elizabeth's steadfast anchor, her strength and stay all these years.

62
00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:37.440
[George] The outspoken, hardworking Duke of Edinburgh had carried out over 22,000 solo public engagements and founded the Duke of Edinburgh's award, which empowered millions of young people across the globe.

63
00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:46.960
[George] On April 9, 2021, Prince Philip passed away peacefully at Windsor Castle at the age of 99, just two months shy of his centenary.

64
00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:48.160
[George] His funeral at St.

65
00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:55.120
[George] George's Chapel on April 17 took place amid the strict isolation protocols of the COVID-19 pandemic.

66
00:08:55.120 --> 00:09:07.560
[George] In one of the most haunting and indelible photographic images in modern royal history, Queen Elizabeth sat entirely alone in the choir of the chapel, dressed in mourning black and wearing a black face mask.

67
00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:12.040
[George] Her head bowed in solitary grief before the coffin of her beloved husband.

68
00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:30.360
[George] By refusing any special royal exemption from the laws she had asked her subjects to obey, Elizabeth demonstrated the supreme moral integrity of her constitutional reign, embodying the quiet, dignified sorrow of millions of families worldwide who had lost loved ones during the global pandemic.

69
00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:45.400
[George] In accordance with Prince Philip's wishes, his coffin was carried to the chapel on a custom land rover defender hearse that the Duke had personally spent sixteen years helping to design and modify, complete with military green paint and open rear stops.

70
00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:52.840
[George] The stark, unvarnished simplicity of his funeral stood as a testament to the selfless ethos of the wartime generation.

71
00:09:52.840 --> 00:10:08.280
[George] In June 2022, Queen Elizabeth II became the first British monarch in history to celebrate a platinum jubilee, marking an astonishing seventy years on the throne, surpassed in verified world history only by King Louis XIV of France.

72
00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:25.720
[George] Though restricted in her mobility, the 96-year-old Queen enchanted the world by appearing in another heartwarming comedy sketch, having afternoon tea with Paddington Bear at Buckingham Palace, mischievously revealing that she kept a marmalade sandwich in her famous handbag, for later.

73
00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:41.960
[George] When she stepped onto the palace balcony dressed in dove grey and leaning on her walking stick, flanked by three generations of heirs, Prince Charles, Prince William, and eight-year old Prince George, a roaring crowd of millions showered her with thunderous love and gratitude.

74
00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:49.120
[George] Three months later, at her beloved Scottish estate of Balmoral, the Queen performed her final constitutional act.

75
00:10:49.280 --> 00:11:00.240
[George] On September 6, 2022, smiling warmly despite purple bruising on her frail hands, she officially appointed Liz Truss as her fifteenth British Prime Minister.

76
00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:05.280
[George] Two days later, on September 8, 2022, Queen Elizabeth I.

77
00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:24.320
[George] Her historic ten-day state funeral brought 500 foreign heads of state to Westminster Abbey, while over 250,000 citizens waited in a five-mile queue along the Thames for up to 24 hours to file past her coffin in Westminster Hall.

78
00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:34.960
[George] Her reign had spanned fifteen British prime ministers, fourteen American presidents and seven popes, an immortal anchor of constancy in a rapidly changing world.

79
00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:37.200
[George] Her final official photograph.

80
00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:51.280
[George] Taken by Press Association photographer Jane Barlow in the drawing room at Balmoral beside a crackling coal fireplace just 48 hours before her death, she showed the tiny monarch smiling warmly, her eyes sparkling with wit.

81
00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:58.000
[George] Embodying until her final breath the vow she had made at twenty-one to serve her people faithfully to the very end.

82
00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:09.360
[George] Upon his mother's passing, seventy-three-year-old Charles immediately acceded to the throne as King Charles III, having served the longest apprenticeship as heir apparent in British history.

83
00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:14.240
[George] For more than five decades as Prince of Wales, Charles had been a passionate.

84
00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:27.440
[George] Visionary pioneer on climate change, organic farming, sustainable urban architecture, and interfaith dialogue, founding the Prince's Trust to help over a million disadvantaged young people build careers.

85
00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:41.640
[George] In his first televised address to the nation, as sovereign, King Charles spoke with profound emotion, promising to uphold the constitutional principles at the heart of the nation with the same unswerving devotion displayed by his beloved mother.

86
00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:47.800
[George] As the Queen herself did with such unswerving devotion, I too now solemnly pledge myself.

87
00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:54.520
[George] Throughout the remaining time God grants me to uphold the constitutional principles at the heart of our nation.

88
00:12:54.520 --> 00:13:01.000
[George] Beside him stood Queen Camilla, whose steadfast loyalty and warmth had earned the respect of the British public.

89
00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:17.720
[George] On May 6, 2023, King Charles III and Queen Camilla were crowned at Westminster Abbey in a majestic, modernized ceremony that incorporated ancient Saxon traditions alongside multifaith blessings, female clergy, and contemporary gospel music.

90
00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:42.440
[George] From King Egbert in 827 and Alfred the Great defending Wessex to King Charles III entering the 21st century, the thousand-year story of the kings and queens of England stands as a magnificent chronicle of war, constitutional courage, cultural brilliance, and enduring democratic evolution, a golden thread connecting an ancient island realm to the modern world.

91
00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:59.320
[Rebecca] We hope you've enjoyed every twist, battle, romance, and constitutional triumph of this thousand-year royal saga.

92
00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:08.800
[Rebecca] Subscribe to most fascinating stories for upcoming deep dive's into legendary figures, ancient empires, and the greatest stories in human history.

93
00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:14.160
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca, and from all of us at Most Fascinating Stories, thank you for listening.

